WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.595
- He is a Canadian businessman, investor,

00:00:02.595 --> 00:00:06.502
entrepreneur, journalist
and television personality.

00:00:06.502 --> 00:00:09.278
He's the co-founder and
chairman of O'Leary Funds

00:00:09.278 --> 00:00:11.399
and the co-founder of Softkey.

00:00:11.399 --> 00:00:14.786
His estimated net worth is $400 million.

00:00:14.786 --> 00:00:16.045
He's Kevin O'Leary and here

00:00:16.045 --> 00:00:18.795
are his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.012
- I tried working for
somebody when I was very young

00:00:24.012 --> 00:00:25.138
and they fired me.

00:00:25.138 --> 00:00:25.971
- Right, in ice cream.

00:00:25.971 --> 00:00:28.178
- Right, and it was a
very, very profoundly,

00:00:28.178 --> 00:00:29.648
affected me profoundly and I just said

00:00:29.648 --> 00:00:31.162
I'm never working for anybody again.

00:00:31.162 --> 00:00:32.616
It doesn't mean your life is easier,

00:00:32.616 --> 00:00:35.655
in fact you have to have a
lot of sacrifices on the road

00:00:35.655 --> 00:00:38.784
to success and a lot of
failures along the way.

00:00:38.784 --> 00:00:41.640
I just find it a more interesting
way to spend your time

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.807
and I really believe in this
concept of money buys freedom.

00:00:47.462 --> 00:00:51.735
I think it's a very noble
pursuit for some percentage of

00:00:51.735 --> 00:00:54.390
the people out there,
but not for everybody,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.956
because you have to sacrifice
a lot for the period

00:00:56.956 --> 00:00:58.736
that you're working on your business,

00:00:58.736 --> 00:01:00.309
and not everybody wants to do that.

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:01.844
I think you find that in your own,

00:01:01.844 --> 00:01:04.668
you start down that path and
if it's too much of a sacrifice

00:01:04.668 --> 00:01:07.125
you revert back to being an employee.

00:01:07.125 --> 00:01:10.378
It's very noble to do that too,
but it's not the same thing.

00:01:10.378 --> 00:01:12.613
It takes a long time to set yourself free,

00:01:12.613 --> 00:01:14.318
working for somebody else.

00:01:14.318 --> 00:01:16.571
I was with a bunch of other
investors who I respect

00:01:16.571 --> 00:01:19.467
and they brought me a deal
and I wanted to be part of it,

00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:21.550
but it didn't feel right.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.525
And I wrote millions
of dollars on that one

00:01:25.525 --> 00:01:27.677
and it just didn't feel
right and I lost it all

00:01:27.677 --> 00:01:28.510
within two years.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:30.241
- So should investors
trust their gut then?

00:01:30.241 --> 00:01:32.487
- Absolutely trust your gut.

00:01:32.487 --> 00:01:34.243
- What about, you said
earlier, don't be emotional

00:01:34.243 --> 00:01:35.895
and sometimes the gut is emotion?

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:38.190
- No, this is a barometer of risk.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:41.250
It's something internalized
for an investor that says,

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.782
I don't feel comfortable.

00:01:42.782 --> 00:01:45.444
It's not about emotion, it's
just don't write that check.

00:01:45.444 --> 00:01:48.280
That was recently the first
time I didn't listen to that

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.650
and I lost everything.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.483
So now my radar is up.

00:01:54.465 --> 00:01:56.923
I listen to my gut. It's not an emotion.

00:01:56.923 --> 00:02:00.854
It's an index of risk
and you get I as you age.

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:02.917
You become a better investor every year.

00:02:02.917 --> 00:02:05.375
If only I could live to
200, I'd be really good.

00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:06.828
Diversification.

00:02:06.828 --> 00:02:09.480
What I've learned that
works if you're an investor,

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:10.859
no matter how much money you got,

00:02:10.859 --> 00:02:14.841
whether it's $5000 or 500
million, diversification is

00:02:14.841 --> 00:02:17.364
what saves you from
disaster, having investments

00:02:17.364 --> 00:02:20.180
in a wide range of businesses and sectors.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:23.590
There's always something working
when something else isn't.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.788
So, today I can go to
bed tonight knowing that

00:02:26.788 --> 00:02:29.139
I have one objective, I
want to go to bed richer

00:02:29.139 --> 00:02:31.394
than I woke up, and the only
way that's going to happen to me

00:02:31.394 --> 00:02:34.230
is to be invested in a
lot of different things.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.562
Because everyday one thing
works while three others don't,

00:02:37.562 --> 00:02:40.711
but sometimes that's why you get richer.

00:02:40.711 --> 00:02:41.645
- So how does that play out for

00:02:41.645 --> 00:02:43.921
the average working woman like myself?

00:02:43.921 --> 00:02:46.969
Does it start with doing an
investor profile questionnaire

00:02:46.969 --> 00:02:49.243
and starting to invest a
little bit of your money

00:02:49.243 --> 00:02:51.183
in a diversified portfolio?

00:02:51.183 --> 00:02:53.795
- Yes, what I find people
do is they fall in love

00:02:53.795 --> 00:02:56.395
with one idea or one
stock or one investment

00:02:56.395 --> 00:02:58.814
or their sister comes in and
says I want to open a restaurant.

00:02:58.814 --> 00:02:59.955
- Ah, yes.

00:02:59.955 --> 00:03:00.990
- You should never do that.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.121
What you should say is
whatever money I've got,

00:03:03.121 --> 00:03:06.464
I'll never put more than 5% into one idea,

00:03:06.464 --> 00:03:08.982
I'll never let myself
get caught up in an idea

00:03:08.982 --> 00:03:10.844
that ends up being so big to me,

00:03:10.844 --> 00:03:12.815
that if it fails I get wiped out.

00:03:12.815 --> 00:03:14.704
This is the key to success.

00:03:14.704 --> 00:03:18.572
No more than 5% in any one
idea and no more than 20%

00:03:18.572 --> 00:03:19.864
in any one sector.

00:03:19.864 --> 00:03:22.888
For example, if you love
energy stocks only 20% max.

00:03:22.888 --> 00:03:25.115
If you love gold only 20% max.

00:03:25.115 --> 00:03:27.115
You have to have diversification.

00:03:27.115 --> 00:03:30.285
This is the one thing
that will save your hiney.

00:03:30.285 --> 00:03:32.491
You should make the assumption
when you make an investment,

00:03:32.491 --> 00:03:34.705
there's some probability you'll lose it.

00:03:34.705 --> 00:03:36.065
It'll go to zero.

00:03:36.065 --> 00:03:38.648
And that's why you need to have

00:03:39.779 --> 00:03:42.995
a reserve, like a backup plan,

00:03:42.995 --> 00:03:47.110
some cash set aside that if
everything blows up you're okay.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.593
I find it extraordinary when
people make their first,

00:03:50.593 --> 00:03:53.531
liquidity event and
they have some capital,

00:03:53.531 --> 00:03:55.326
that they blow it all again.

00:03:55.326 --> 00:03:56.780
That is the biggest mistake.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:59.910
You've got to take a nut,
even if it stays in cash,

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.723
or it's a very, very liquid
safe security to say,

00:04:02.723 --> 00:04:04.015
that I don't touch.

00:04:04.015 --> 00:04:06.035
It goes back to what my mother taught me,

00:04:06.035 --> 00:04:08.809
spend the interest never the principal.

00:04:08.809 --> 00:04:10.719
I mean look, I make a
lot of crazy investments,

00:04:10.719 --> 00:04:12.394
I don't touch the principal.

00:04:12.394 --> 00:04:15.035
Communication skills it
turns out are paramount.

00:04:15.035 --> 00:04:17.494
The ability to actually
articulate what your idea is

00:04:17.494 --> 00:04:19.699
in a very short period
of time is number one.

00:04:19.699 --> 00:04:22.735
Number two, and this is far
in some ways more complicated

00:04:22.735 --> 00:04:24.828
to explain but, just
'cause you have a good idea

00:04:24.828 --> 00:04:27.086
doesn't make it you're
going to make money.

00:04:27.086 --> 00:04:28.723
There's a difference between a good idea

00:04:28.723 --> 00:04:31.027
and someone who can
execute the business plan.

00:04:31.027 --> 00:04:33.294
So you spend the next five
minutes of your presentation

00:04:33.294 --> 00:04:34.928
explaining why you're the right person

00:04:34.928 --> 00:04:37.064
to actually execute the plan,

00:04:37.064 --> 00:04:38.426
and this is far more complicated

00:04:38.426 --> 00:04:40.531
because you get peppered with
a whole series of questions

00:04:40.531 --> 00:04:42.625
in any presentation that you're
trying to raise money for.

00:04:42.625 --> 00:04:43.954
Whether it's on Dragons Den, Shark Tank

00:04:43.954 --> 00:04:45.192
or else somewhere else.

00:04:45.192 --> 00:04:49.270
How much of your own money have
you put into this business?

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:50.942
- I've put $50,000 of my own money

00:04:50.942 --> 00:04:52.750
and I haven't taken a
salary out of this company

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:53.583
in a year and a half.

00:04:53.583 --> 00:04:54.789
- Well cry me a river.

00:04:54.789 --> 00:04:56.545
You have to go through
that gauntlet to prove

00:04:56.545 --> 00:04:57.654
that you're the right person

00:04:57.654 --> 00:04:59.909
or your team are the right people.

00:04:59.909 --> 00:05:01.725
And if those two things come together,

00:05:01.725 --> 00:05:03.229
then the magic starts.

00:05:03.229 --> 00:05:05.956
That's when a team or a
person starts to sizzle

00:05:05.956 --> 00:05:08.666
like an isotope, because
clearly they've become a leader.

00:05:08.666 --> 00:05:11.085
It's clear that they're
different than everybody else.

00:05:11.085 --> 00:05:12.549
Not only can they articulate their idea,

00:05:12.549 --> 00:05:14.559
they can actually convince
you they can execute it.

00:05:14.559 --> 00:05:16.827
The reason most people fail in business

00:05:16.827 --> 00:05:19.721
is they make the assumption
they're good at everything.

00:05:19.721 --> 00:05:22.304
And they're egotistical maniacs

00:05:23.419 --> 00:05:25.482
and even when it starts to fail

00:05:25.482 --> 00:05:29.341
because of their own inadequacies
they don't stop themselves

00:05:29.341 --> 00:05:30.765
and they blow up.

00:05:30.765 --> 00:05:33.548
That's a typical situation
for an entrepreneur.

00:05:33.548 --> 00:05:35.426
So you have to look yourself in the mirror

00:05:35.426 --> 00:05:37.945
and this is hard to do sometimes, and say,

00:05:37.945 --> 00:05:42.468
what am I really good at
and what do I suck at?

00:05:42.468 --> 00:05:45.373
Because you don't want
to spend any of your time

00:05:45.373 --> 00:05:46.741
doing things you're no good at.

00:05:46.741 --> 00:05:50.824
I'll give you an example, I
hate logistics and operations.

00:05:50.824 --> 00:05:52.907
If you got a software company
you have to make 10 million

00:05:52.907 --> 00:05:55.436
discs a month and you don't like that,

00:05:55.436 --> 00:05:58.573
you better find a guy
who knows how to do that,

00:05:58.573 --> 00:05:59.874
and you better make him your partner

00:05:59.874 --> 00:06:02.230
very early on in the process.

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:04.854
And I mean partner, I mean
you give him something.

00:06:04.854 --> 00:06:07.901
You give equity up to
fulfill the weaknesses

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:09.791
in your own portfolio.

00:06:09.791 --> 00:06:11.103
That is the key to success.

00:06:11.103 --> 00:06:15.845
I will never make any
money doing by myself ever.

00:06:15.845 --> 00:06:17.686
I've sold many businesses now,

00:06:17.686 --> 00:06:19.616
started many, failed at many,

00:06:19.616 --> 00:06:23.221
the only ones I've really made
money from are partnerships,

00:06:23.221 --> 00:06:25.368
where I found people
that were good at things

00:06:25.368 --> 00:06:26.668
that I wasn't good at.

00:06:26.668 --> 00:06:29.965
I don't mean employees,
I mean giving them equity

00:06:29.965 --> 00:06:32.763
in the business so that
they have the same risks

00:06:32.763 --> 00:06:35.064
and desires that I have.

00:06:35.064 --> 00:06:38.079
There's only two things
in business that matter.

00:06:38.079 --> 00:06:41.958
One is, owning it and the
other is being an employee.

00:06:41.958 --> 00:06:43.552
Guess which one you want to be.

00:06:43.552 --> 00:06:46.229
And I remembered a story,
really affected me years ago.

00:06:46.229 --> 00:06:48.531
My mother, I was born in
Montréal worked at a company

00:06:48.531 --> 00:06:50.229
called Kitty's Togs and
she used to come home

00:06:50.229 --> 00:06:52.177
and tell my brother and
I, because they'd pay them

00:06:52.177 --> 00:06:53.639
every week on Thursday morning.

00:06:53.639 --> 00:06:55.425
She'd always invest a
third of her paycheck

00:06:55.425 --> 00:06:57.854
into Bell bonds with coupons.

00:06:57.854 --> 00:06:59.358
She'd always say to me,

00:06:59.358 --> 00:07:02.444
"Boys, never spend the
principal, just the interest

00:07:02.444 --> 00:07:05.851
and never ever, ever, ever buy a security

00:07:05.851 --> 00:07:08.015
that doesn't pay a dividend."

00:07:08.015 --> 00:07:11.390
Back in those days stocks
paid higher dividends

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:12.389
than the bonds.

00:07:12.389 --> 00:07:15.091
So example, Bell Canada had a 6% yield

00:07:15.091 --> 00:07:17.367
on its five-year papers, bonds.

00:07:17.367 --> 00:07:19.895
The stock yielded seven to
entice people to take the risk

00:07:19.895 --> 00:07:22.995
to own an equity, and we got
lost somewhere between the 50s

00:07:22.995 --> 00:07:25.441
and the 60s and 70s,
and we actually thought

00:07:25.441 --> 00:07:27.230
that it was a good
investment to buy a stock

00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:29.708
that didn't pay a dividend,
which to me is insane.

00:07:29.708 --> 00:07:33.305
So anyways, I wanted to
found a firm on that,

00:07:33.305 --> 00:07:37.461
my mother's basic concept
because, she died a few years ago

00:07:37.461 --> 00:07:41.281
and I was executor for
her state and I looked at

00:07:41.281 --> 00:07:44.174
her portfolio, which she
never shared with anybody.

00:07:44.174 --> 00:07:47.924
But 40 years, compounded
dividends and bonds,

00:07:50.937 --> 00:07:53.491
73% of the markets
returns in last 40 years

00:07:53.491 --> 00:07:55.703
came from dividends not
capital appreciation

00:07:55.703 --> 00:07:57.785
and she knew that intuitively.

00:07:57.785 --> 00:08:00.745
So that's how we built the
foundation of O'Leary funds.

00:08:00.745 --> 00:08:02.405
We listened to mom.

00:08:02.405 --> 00:08:06.199
We don't own a single security
that doesn't pay a dividend.

00:08:06.199 --> 00:08:08.352
I will never buy a stock in my life

00:08:08.352 --> 00:08:09.327
that doesn't pay a dividend.

00:08:09.327 --> 00:08:11.907
It's not returning cash
to me it's a speculation,

00:08:11.907 --> 00:08:15.484
and if a manager can't send
me cash, I'm not interested.

00:08:15.484 --> 00:08:18.042
Often, your family will
come to you, your cousin,

00:08:18.042 --> 00:08:21.268
your uncle, your sister saying
please invest in my business.

00:08:21.268 --> 00:08:23.550
You've got to differentiate
between family,

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:25.277
friends and money.

00:08:25.277 --> 00:08:27.482
You've got to be careful
that you don't get yourself

00:08:27.482 --> 00:08:29.757
in a situation where you lose your money

00:08:29.757 --> 00:08:31.761
because you got emotionally involved.

00:08:31.761 --> 00:08:34.669
That's a very big piece of
advice, because I've done it the,

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:36.486
everybody's made mistakes as an investor.

00:08:36.486 --> 00:08:38.244
That's one of the ones I
made in the early times

00:08:38.244 --> 00:08:39.716
I never make again.

00:08:39.716 --> 00:08:42.416
But I tell every Canadian
entrepreneur I talk to

00:08:42.416 --> 00:08:46.583
and business leader and owner
of business, any business

00:08:48.242 --> 00:08:51.378
try and get over the next
five years 30% of your sales

00:08:51.378 --> 00:08:54.040
outside of North America.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:55.650
And Canada is unique.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:59.817
We actually have within the
government a support system.

00:09:02.262 --> 00:09:04.458
You can call the consulate
in any country you want

00:09:04.458 --> 00:09:06.702
to do business in,
they'll return your call,

00:09:06.702 --> 00:09:08.298
they'll set up meetings for you.

00:09:08.298 --> 00:09:11.491
I did this two decades ago when
I wanted to move to states,

00:09:11.491 --> 00:09:13.889
I called the Canadian consulate in Boston.

00:09:13.889 --> 00:09:17.148
You can do that now in Brazil,
India, China, Asia, Cambodia,

00:09:17.148 --> 00:09:19.221
Thailand, and there's
somebody that will say,

00:09:19.221 --> 00:09:20.858
I care about that Canadian company,

00:09:20.858 --> 00:09:22.167
I'm going to make calls for them.

00:09:22.167 --> 00:09:23.344
You still got to do the work

00:09:23.344 --> 00:09:26.129
but the whole idea is you
diversify your sales risk,

00:09:26.129 --> 00:09:27.969
you diversify your portfolio.

00:09:27.969 --> 00:09:30.407
If you're just selling in the
US, you're going to be selling

00:09:30.407 --> 00:09:33.059
to an economy that's growing at sub 3%

00:09:33.059 --> 00:09:34.238
for the rest of your life.

00:09:34.238 --> 00:09:38.531
So, Oprah has a hairdresser.

00:09:38.531 --> 00:09:41.448
Interesting guy, his hobby was tea.

00:09:44.017 --> 00:09:46.921
This tea you drink and he
became what's called a nose.

00:09:46.921 --> 00:09:50.417
He had a certain skill to
be able to smell sulon teas

00:09:50.417 --> 00:09:51.717
and blend them.

00:09:51.717 --> 00:09:54.042
One day he gives a couple of
years ago a cup of tea to Oprah

00:09:54.042 --> 00:09:55.810
while he's doing her hair,

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:58.022
and she sips it and says this
is the best tea I've ever had.

00:09:58.022 --> 00:10:00.138
She goes on the show a half
an hour later and says,

00:10:00.138 --> 00:10:04.578
"This tea, called Talbot Teas
is the best tea I've ever had.

00:10:04.578 --> 00:10:07.797
He gets orders the next day for $500,000.

00:10:07.797 --> 00:10:10.125
You know the Oprah effect,
you've heard of it right?

00:10:10.125 --> 00:10:11.861
He can't finance that.

00:10:11.861 --> 00:10:16.403
So he comes on Shark Tank
and he's looking for $250,000

00:10:16.403 --> 00:10:19.175
and I agree to buy 35%
of the company for that.

00:10:19.175 --> 00:10:20.893
I love tea, it's so simple.

00:10:20.893 --> 00:10:23.420
Gets on Oprah, 5000 orders, Neiman Marcus,

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:26.501
all kinds of individuals
ordering on the internet, simple.

00:10:26.501 --> 00:10:28.918
Tea, orders, cash flow right?

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:32.495
While we're in the
middle of doing the deal,

00:10:32.495 --> 00:10:35.139
the show airs and Jamba
Juice, a public company

00:10:35.139 --> 00:10:36.834
says we want to be in the tea business.

00:10:36.834 --> 00:10:38.479
We love this story, we love the brand,

00:10:38.479 --> 00:10:39.994
I want to buy the company.

00:10:39.994 --> 00:10:41.518
I haven't even written the check yet

00:10:41.518 --> 00:10:43.631
and bang, the deal's done.

00:10:43.631 --> 00:10:44.706
- Wow.

00:10:44.706 --> 00:10:46.253
- Yes, wow.

00:10:46.253 --> 00:10:49.039
That's the kind of deal I
like, because it was simple.

00:10:49.039 --> 00:10:50.636
It's not a complicated text story.

00:10:50.636 --> 00:10:52.586
It's tea, tea with a brand.

00:10:52.586 --> 00:10:55.248
Now you go into a Jamba Juice
anywhere in North America,

00:10:55.248 --> 00:10:56.833
that's my Talbot Teas.

00:10:56.833 --> 00:10:58.223
- Wow.
- That's right.

00:10:58.223 --> 00:10:59.169
- Isn't that wonderful?

00:10:59.169 --> 00:11:00.740
It is wonderful, that's why you are?

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:02.971
- Well, that's why I am Mr. Wonderful.

00:11:02.971 --> 00:11:05.210
But I look at it this
way, the simple deals

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:07.325
are the the ones you make money on.

00:11:07.325 --> 00:11:09.985
So I take that cash back, I redeploy it.

00:11:09.985 --> 00:11:13.481
So I like simple stories with
revenue attached to them.

00:11:13.481 --> 00:11:16.549
The high tech stuff, I
came out of that market,

00:11:16.549 --> 00:11:18.478
it's so complicated, so much can go wrong,

00:11:18.478 --> 00:11:21.619
so I just love deals where they,

00:11:21.619 --> 00:11:23.983
see an easy path to revenue.

00:11:23.983 --> 00:11:25.062
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:25.062 --> 00:11:28.534
I made this video because
Adil Albert asked me to,

00:11:28.534 --> 00:11:30.617
so if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:11:30.617 --> 00:11:33.209
to profile next, leave
it in the comments below

00:11:33.209 --> 00:11:34.449
and I'll see what I can do.

00:11:34.449 --> 00:11:37.325
I'd also love to know which of
Kevin O'Leary's top 10 rules

00:11:37.325 --> 00:11:39.476
had the biggest impact on you.

00:11:39.476 --> 00:11:41.702
Leave it in the comments and
I'll join in the discussion.

00:11:41.702 --> 00:11:42.894
Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:42.894 --> 00:11:46.394
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

00:11:51.307 --> 00:11:53.974
You know they're probably
in their 50s, 60s, 70s

00:11:53.974 --> 00:11:57.376
and even older, their number one mandate

00:11:57.376 --> 00:12:00.835
to their own investment strategy
is preservation of wealth.

00:12:00.835 --> 00:12:02.248
Secondly it's income,

00:12:02.248 --> 00:12:05.752
and lastly on the hit parade
is capital appreciation.

00:12:05.752 --> 00:12:07.580
The only way we get capital appreciation

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:10.181
is we find companies that
are growing free cash flow.

00:12:10.181 --> 00:12:13.057
I don't believe in earnings.
That's mumbo-jumbo to me.

00:12:13.057 --> 00:12:16.603
I believe in free cash after cap X.

00:12:16.603 --> 00:12:18.981
You tell me how much free
cash flow you're making

00:12:18.981 --> 00:12:21.614
every quarter, it's the
only thing I care about.

00:12:21.614 --> 00:12:22.873
I don't want to hear anything else

00:12:22.873 --> 00:12:25.576
because if you're not,
you can't lie about cash.

00:12:25.576 --> 00:12:27.311
That's what I love about cash.

00:12:27.311 --> 00:12:31.404
There is no vision, there's
no story, there's just cash.

00:12:31.404 --> 00:12:33.629
That's what I trust and
that's what I understand.

00:12:33.629 --> 00:12:35.682
So I find companies that grow in cash,

00:12:35.682 --> 00:12:37.847
I buy their debt, their
prefs, their converts,

00:12:37.847 --> 00:12:39.888
their common shares because
they're paying dividend

00:12:39.888 --> 00:12:41.545
and I wait in the weeds.

00:12:41.545 --> 00:12:44.064
I get paid every single quarter.

00:12:44.064 --> 00:12:47.051
Anything that doesn't pay
me cash, not interested.

00:12:47.051 --> 00:12:49.051
When it comes to winning in business,

00:12:49.051 --> 00:12:50.247
I like to be very competitive.

00:12:50.247 --> 00:12:52.517
I like to pour boiling
oil over my competitors

00:12:52.517 --> 00:12:55.084
and think everyday how can I kill them?

00:12:55.084 --> 00:12:56.763
Because that is the war of business.

00:12:56.763 --> 00:12:58.835
Ultimately, I want my
shareholders to make money

00:12:58.835 --> 00:13:01.280
than my competitors,
and that's very healthy.

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:03.555
So I think the DNA of
a business has to be,

00:13:03.555 --> 00:13:05.649
how do you make money,
how do you spend your time

00:13:05.649 --> 00:13:06.971
making money for your shareholders?

00:13:06.971 --> 00:13:08.701
You're nobody as an entrepreneur
until you've made money

00:13:08.701 --> 00:13:09.977
for somebody else.

00:13:09.977 --> 00:13:14.048
I'd like to be remembered
for sticking with one ideal,

00:13:14.048 --> 00:13:17.510
supporting capitalism, believing
in it and fighting for it

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:18.819
my whole life.

00:13:18.819 --> 00:13:20.946
I mean I really, really, really believe

00:13:20.946 --> 00:13:25.053
that the only value in a society
is that of an entrepreneur

00:13:25.053 --> 00:13:27.999
creating a company, creating
jobs, creating wealth

00:13:27.999 --> 00:13:30.019
and sustaining our standard of living.

00:13:30.019 --> 00:13:34.198
Government gets us nothing,
there is no value created there.

00:13:34.198 --> 00:13:36.340
You pay for it from the private sector

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:38.108
and it provides a service.

00:13:38.108 --> 00:13:41.288
As far as I'm concerned,
every government employee

00:13:41.288 --> 00:13:44.873
works for me and that
we should ask from them

00:13:44.873 --> 00:13:46.986
the same metrics we put on ourselves.

00:13:46.986 --> 00:13:48.612
If you don't perform you should be fired,

00:13:48.612 --> 00:13:49.720
we do that in the private sector.

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:52.086
We should do it in public sector as well.

00:13:52.086 --> 00:13:55.308
I respect them as leaders
but I also understand I pay

00:13:55.308 --> 00:13:57.310
for all of their costs.

00:13:57.310 --> 00:14:00.551
So what I want and what I
want to be remembered for

00:14:00.551 --> 00:14:04.718
is to my dying day, I want
to cut government by a third.

00:14:05.896 --> 00:14:09.269
I believe that every dollar
they spend is 33% wasted

00:14:09.269 --> 00:14:11.663
and they're 33% too big.

00:14:11.663 --> 00:14:15.273
In this country we need less government.

00:14:15.273 --> 00:14:17.660
We need more private sector.

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:20.109
That's how we become rich and win.

